a. Of or pertaining to the group of deer of which the axis, or spotted Indian hog-deer, is the type.

'''II.''' ''n.'' A deer of the axine group.

'''axinite''' (ak′si-nīt), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀξίνη, ax (see ''ax''<sup>1</sup>), + ''-ite''<sup>2</sup>.] A mineral occurring commonly in crystals, whose general form is that of a very oblique rhomb, so flattened that some of its edges become thin and sharp like the edge of an ax (whence its name), also sometimes found in lamellar masses. {{smaller|It is a silicate of aluminium, iron, and manganese and calcium, with 5 per cent. of boron trioxid, and is commonly of a clove-brown or plum-blue color.}}

'''axinomancy''' (ak′sin- or ak-sin′ọ̄-man-si), ''n.'' [< L. ''axinomantia'', < Gr. *ἀξινομαντεία, < ἀξίνη, ax, + μαντεία, divination: see ''Mantis''.] An ancient kind of divination for the detection of crime by means of an ax or axes. {{smaller|One form consisted in poising an ax on a bar, and repeating the names of persons suspected. If the ax moved at the name of any one, he was pronounced guilty. For another form, see extract.}}
{{smaller block|[Jet] was moreover employed in the form of divination called ''axinomancy''. Laid on a hatchet made hot, it was stated not to consume if the desires of the consulting party were destined to be fulfilled.
{{right|''Archæologia'', XLIII. 517. (''Davies' Sup. Gloss.'')}}}}

'''axinometry''', ''n.'' See ''axonometry''.

'''axiolite''' (ak′si-ọ̄-līt), ''n.'' [< L. ''axis'', axis, + Gr. λίθος, stone.] An aggregation of rudimentary crystal-fibers and products of devitrification, occurring in certain rocks like rhyolite. {{smaller|Axiolites resemble spherulites, except that their arrangement is divergent from a line instead of from a point.}}

